In Python, functions are first-class objects. A higher-order function is a function that accepts or returns another function. A decorator uses this concept to modify or enhance function execution. Example:
def my_decorator(func):
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        print('Before execution')
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        print('After execution')
        return result
    return wrapper
@my_decorator
def say_hello(): print('Hello')
Decorators are widely used for logging, access control, caching (@lru_cache), and routing (Flask/Django).

Singleton Pattern ensures a class has only one instance in memory and provides a global access point. Thread-safe double-checked locking implementation:
public class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton instance;
    private Singleton() {} // private constructor
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) { // 1st check (no locking)
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) { // 2nd check (with locking)
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
The 'volatile' keyword is mandatory to prevent instruction reordering during object allocation. Alternative: Bill Pugh static inner class holder (lazy + thread-safe without explicit synchronization) or Enum singleton.

When overriding a method in a subclass, covariant return type allows replacing the superclass method's return type with a subtype of that return type. Example in Java:
class Animal { Animal reproduce() { return new Animal(); } }
class Dog extends Animal { @Override Dog reproduce() { return new Dog(); } }
Here Dog's reproduce() returns Dog, which is a subtype of Animal. Benefits: eliminates unnecessary explicit type casting in client code (e.g., Dog d = (Dog) animal.reproduce() becomes Dog d = dog.reproduce()). Supported in Java 5+ and C++. The return type must be a subtype; widening to a broader type is forbidden.

Inversion of Control (IoC) flips the traditional control flow: instead of application code instantiating its dependencies, an external framework (e.g. Spring IoC Container) manages component lifecycles. Dependency Injection (DI) is the mechanism to implement IoC. Types of DI: (1) Constructor Injection (preferred): dependencies passed via constructor. (2) Setter Injection: passed via setter methods. (3) Field Injection (@Autowired). Example:
class Car {
  private final Engine engine;
  public Car(Engine engine) { this.engine = engine; } // DI via constructor
}
Benefits: (1) Loose coupling — Car works with any Engine implementation (ElectricEngine, V8Engine). (2) Easy unit testing — mock objects can be injected effortlessly.

Naive set bit counting loops through all 32 or 64 bits (O(32) / O(64)). Brian Kernighan's algorithm uses the bitwise property that subtracting 1 from a number flips all bits after the rightmost set bit (inclusive). Performing `n = n & (n - 1)` clears the rightmost set bit of n in one step. Algorithm:
int count = 0;
while (n > 0) {
    n = n & (n - 1);
    count++;
}
return count;
Time Complexity: O(K) where K is the number of set bits (1s) in n. Best case: O(1) for powers of 2. Space complexity: O(1). Also used to check if a number is a power of 2: `(n > 0) && ((n & (n - 1)) == 0)`.

To shift all 0s to the end of an integer array in-place while keeping non-zero elements in original relative order:
Algorithm:
int writeIndex = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] != 0) {
        arr[writeIndex] = arr[i];
        writeIndex++;
    }
}
while (writeIndex < arr.length) {
    arr[writeIndex] = 0;
    writeIndex++;
}
Time Complexity: O(n) single pass through array.
Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary memory (in-place modification).

A palindrome reads identical forwards and backwards. To check if a string is a palindrome considering only alphanumeric characters and ignoring case:
Algorithm (Two-Pointer In-Place):
int left = 0, right = str.length() - 1;
while (left < right) {
    while (left < right && !isAlphanumeric(str[left])) left++;
    while (left < right && !isAlphanumeric(str[right])) right--;
    if (toLowerCase(str[left]) != toLowerCase(str[right])) return false;
    left++; right--;
}
return true;
Time Complexity: O(n) linear scan.
Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space when pointers skip non-alphanumeric chars directly without building new strings.

To find the second largest distinct element in an unsorted array in a single pass (O(n) time, O(1) space):
Algorithm:
int first = Integer.MIN_VALUE, second = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
for (int num : arr) {
    if (num > first) {
        second = first;
        first = num;
    } else if (num > second && num != first) {
        second = num;
    }
}
if (second == Integer.MIN_VALUE) return -1; // No second largest exists
return second;
Why better than sorting: sorting takes O(n log n) time and modifies or copies the array. Single pass takes strictly O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.

The Factory Method pattern decouples the client code from the concrete classes it needs to instantiate. Problem: client code uses 'new ConcreteProduct()', creating tight coupling to concrete implementations. Solution: define a factory interface or abstract method `createProduct()` in a creator class. Subclasses override `createProduct()` to return specific instances. Example:
interface Document { void open(); }
class PdfDocument implements Document { public void open() { ... } }
class WordDocument implements Document { public void open() { ... } }
abstract class Application {
    abstract Document createDocument();
    public void openDocument() { Document doc = createDocument(); doc.open(); }
}
class PdfApp extends Application { Document createDocument() { return new PdfDocument(); } }
Benefits: adheres to Open/Closed Principle (add new Document types without modifying client app logic).
